@NickLucche Just to clarify: you’d still be in favour of just making NixlConnector and NixlPullConnector behave the same and otherwise leaving the interleave handling alone?

Because the smallest fix is probably basically my original #60168 and what this PR then picked up in the first place. I only suggested the more centralised connector-level refactor because once we start adding more special cases here, it feels cleaner long term to put that policy on the connector and have MultiConnector delegate it.

Just want to make sure we’re not confusing @DimensionSTP

@NickLucche, I removed the optional alias-matching argument. Both NIXL pull names now always return the same result from has_connector(), including inside MultiConnector. Other connector names still match literally.

The auxiliary-output check now lists only NixlPullConnector, and the legacy name reports that canonical name in the error message. I also restored the original nixl_pd_active variable name and comment, and removed the exact-name tests for the deleted argument.

The focused configuration tests and the real EngineArgs configuration checks pass. Explicit interleave validation is retained; I have explained that part separately in the interleave review thread.

@NickLucche @lucamotz, I have kept this revision limited to the alias fix and the existing explicit-value validation, without introducing connector-owned policy or changing MultiConnector delegation.

The auto-resolution flag already exists upstream: EngineArgs sets it according to whether the option was omitted, and the worker adjustment already checks it. I retained it in validate_block_size() because normalizing the aliases without it would exempt explicit values under both names. With block size 16, an omitted value resolves to 16, explicit 1 and 8 remain unchanged, and explicit 3 or 32 is rejected under both names.

#60483 and #60484 add complementary checks between peers: asymmetric-DCP block alignment and matching interleave sizes. Those do not replace this local block-size check. For example, explicit 8 can pass local validation with block size 16 but still be rejected for an asymmetric-DCP transfer by #60483.

